# Alex — the deck, what I changed, and what to know before the first meeting

**TL;DR.** Lead with Autopilot. Since May, MRR is up {{ m.rev_growth_apr_oct }} and Autopilot plans brought {{ m.rev_drvg_autopilot_share }} of the new revenue. Monthly logo churn also fell from {{ m.fmt('ret_logo_churn_avg_pre', decimals=1) }} to {{ m.fmt('ret_logo_churn_avg_post', decimals=1) }}. Pitch **{{ m.rev_arr_post_oct26 }} ARR and {{ m.rev_paying_customers_oct26 }} paying customers**. Three things not to say: "$6.0M ARR / 344 customers", "no competitors", "Meta is our best channel".

## What I changed from your notes, and why
- **ARR {{ m.rev_kpi_arr_oct26 }} → {{ m.rev_arr_post_oct26 }}.** The KPI-sheet "MRR" is Stripe cash collected: it includes annual prepay, one-time setup fees and usage tiers. Diligence rebuilds ARR from subscriptions in the first hour, so the gap would land on us. Appendix A1 bridges the two to the dollar. The customer count drops to paying customers only, because the {{ m.dq_kpi_customers_oct26 }} includes {{ m.rev_subs_trialing_count }} trials.
- **12 slides instead of 25–30.** The 14 features become three jobs plus Autopilot. I cut the TAM slide, since all of global e-commerce is not a market a partner will accept. The data room has no sourced input for a bottom-up SAM, so I left market size out rather than invent one.
- **Added competition and gross margin.** A partner finds Rally AI ({{ m.fmt('in_rally_raise', decimals=0) }} raised) in five minutes, and asks for margin in the first call. Shown openly, the summer dip is a solved problem, not a surprise.
- **Brightline is disclosed, not used as the lead logo.** It is {{ m.rev_top1_share }} of MRR and {{ m.rev_brightline_share_of_delta }} of the growth since April, so the deck frames agencies as a channel and states the concentration. The Little Tea House quote is gone because they churned in August (failed payment).
- **Channels: data instead of claims.** I removed the March TikTok chart: {{ m.ch_tiktok_mar_signups }} signups became {{ m.ch_tiktok_mar_trials }} trials and no paying customers. Meta's own trial count is platform-modelled; our own count is {{ m.ch_meta_trials_internal }}, and Google overstates the same way. The cheapest channel is the App Store ({{ m.ch_cac_app_store }} CAC) against {{ m.ch_cac_meta }} for Meta. Use of funds is now three milestones instead of 50/30/20 with a "buffer".

## Know before the meeting (highest diligence risk first)
1. **Runway is {{ m.burn_runway_months }} months.** Cash is {{ m.burn_cash_oct26 }}, net burn {{ m.burn_avg_3m }} a month (3-month average). To keep 6 months of cash in hand, the round has to close by **{{ m.burn_close_by_month }}**.
2. **Concentration.** Brightline is {{ m.rev_top1_share }} of MRR across {{ m.rev_brightline_sub_brands_oct26 }} brands, so expect "what if they leave". Without Brightline, growth is still {{ m.rev_growth_apr_oct_ex_bl }}.
3. **Margin.** Inference pushed GM to {{ m.der_gm_min }} in {{ m.der_gm_min_month }}. The vendor switch in August cut inference cost per 1k agent actions from {{ m.gm_inference_per_1k_actions_peak }} to {{ m.gm_inference_per_1k_actions_oct26 }}, and GM is back at {{ m.der_gm_oct26 }}.
4. **Autopilot growth came from new customers only.** No customer has ever changed plan. {{ m.der_legacy_customers }} paying customers are still on legacy prices, and moving them to same-tier new prices is worth {{ m.der_legacy_reprice_upside }} MRR. Present that as a milestone, not as something we do not know.
5. **Support load is growing faster than the base.** Tickets per customer are up since Q2, and {{ m.ret_tix_share_agent_related_2026q3 }} of Q3 tickets are about agent behaviour; CS is {{ m.team_hc_customer_success }} of {{ m.team_hc_total }} people. The good news: Autopilot users undo {{ m.fmt("ret_beh_on_undo_rate_oct26", decimals=1) }} of agent actions against {{ m.fmt("ret_beh_off_xbl_undo_rate_oct26", decimals=1) }} for manual users. Undos per customer are higher only because Autopilot runs about three times the actions.
6. **Data hygiene.** KPI cash and burn match the bank within {{ m.burn_kpi_cash_maxdiff }}. KPI MRR, ARR and customers do not match Stripe. March's {{ m.dq_kpi_mar26_customers }} customers counts the trial wave. Two records are dated after the cut-off, including a dispute dated 5 December, and three card disputes were lost as fraud in Sep–Oct.

## Assumptions
ARR is subscription MRR after discounts, ×12. It excludes one-time fees and usage tiers ({{ m.der_usage_arr }} run-rate, shown separately). Paying means billed above $0 in October. Cut-off is 31 Oct 2026. Gross margin counts usage revenue against the same COGS. CAC uses Stripe channel tags and bank spend, never platform attribution.

## Likely partner questions
**Why not $6M?** That figure is cash ×12; A1 walks it to {{ m.rev_arr_post_oct26 }}. **If Brightline leaves?** Growth ex-Brightline is still {{ m.rev_growth_apr_oct_ex_bl }}. **Rally AI?** They buy media; we run ads, SEO and email as one loop. **Is it just the price rise?** No: {{ m.rev_pv_volume_share }} of new MRR is more customers, not higher prices. **If the round slips?** Cash lasts to {{ m.burn_zero_cash_month }}.

**Next questions for us:** why legacy customers never migrated; Brightline contract term; inference cost if agent actions double again; an outcome metric (customer ROAS) we can show.
